{
  "dom_id": "step-q166992",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}?$\n\nI. Its range is $(\\frac{2}{7},\\infty ).$\n\nII. Its domain is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nIII. It passes through the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7}).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14194531/assets/5259fcd21c4fe03e4facb4e8a49860122e051b8427e4342a829f150aa3b30e6e.png)\n\nNow, let's look at each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false, since the range of the function is $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is false, since the domain of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true. $f(1)=\\frac{2}{7}{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}=\\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{3}{7}.$ Therefore, the point $(1,\\frac{3}{7})$ lies on the curve.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true.",
